--TEST--
`rollBack` is a member of the transaction alone, because `rule:core-classes/db-transactions` removes the connection-level one along with `commit` and `inTransaction` — `rule:core-classes/db-transactions`, spec § 18
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The boundary on both sides: `rollBack` is a member of the transaction and of
// nothing else. `rule:core-classes/db-transactions` removes the connection-level `beginTransaction`,
// `commit`, `rollBack` and `inTransaction` outright — nesting is what removed
// the reason each of them existed — so the only place a rollback can be asked
// for is inside the scope that owns one.
var $db = Core\Db::connect("main");

$db->transaction(fn (Core\Db\Transaction $tx): void => {
    // Everything the transaction did is reachable right up to the refusal, and
    // none of it survives it: `rollBack` is the last statement that can run
    // here, because it throws rather than returning.
    var $rows = $tx->query("select id from accounts", []);
    var $write = $tx->execute("update accounts set owner = ? where id = ?", ["ada", 1]);
    var $written = $tx->executeMany("insert into accounts (id) values (?)", [[3], [4]]);
    $tx->rollBack("the row is already gone");
});

// Refused: the same spelling on the connection, which has no scope to abandon
// and so has no such member to reach for.
$db->rollBack("the row is already gone");
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0405]: `Core\Db\Connection` has no method named `rollBack`
   --> case.nvs:21:1
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
